   "FavorStory_151203_Title": "When We Remembered",
   "FavorStory_151204_Content": "\"You're free now.\"\nShe could still hear the cat saying it.\nFree? Free to go where? Something deep in her mind made the choice for her, because her feet had already set out for the one place her memory still held onto: home, the Federated Cities.\n\"Fifth Sector. Miss Lily Grace?\" The official at the border turned her papers over and turned them back. They were a Resonator soldier's identification. He scratched his head and kept turning them, as though a different answer might work its way up to the surface. It was a long time before he found anything to say. \"Welcome back, Miss Grace. Only your country isn't here any more. This is the New Federation now. Welcome to the New Federation for the first time.\"\n\"Not here any more? What does that mean?\"\nShe took it for a joke at first. But there were the strange streets, and the strange buildings, and the strange way people looked at her, and she understood...\nTime in the Somnoire does not keep pace with time in the world. The Federated Cities she remembered had crumbled while she was away.\nShe wanted out from under the stares. She wanted to go home, even if only the thinnest chance of a home was left...\nClinging to that chance, she stumbled through the city, asking strangers, and from the broken pieces she could still describe, she assembled a direction home. But at the junction, Lily found that her house had become a tower burning with light from the pavement to the roof. A restaurant had taken the ground floor. Men and women in fine clothes were arranged along the window, and the lamps lacquered their laughing faces bright, so bright. She stood on the other side of the glass like someone from another world.\n\"Good evening, miss. Will you be dining with us?\"\n\"Our little princess is back. Go and wash your hands. Dinner's ready.\"\nThe waiter's voice fell exactly over her mother's. Her mother used to call her in to dinner from behind that door, in a voice that went up high and long. Now, standing here, she heard the old voice fall and smash on the ground.\nThe night came the whole way down. Lily hauled her case along a street that was taking her nowhere and stood in the gaps between the lamps, where the light drew her shadow out long and wrung it out of shape. And there she understood at last. The age had walked on past the Federated Cities, and shed the soldiers along with them, and nobody anywhere still held the memory that any of them had been here.\nShe folded down onto her heels, buried her face in her hands and cried. It went on for a long time before she got up, took the handle of the case, and walked on. She did not know where she was going. She knew only that she must not stop, because the moment she stopped she would truly have nowhere left to go.",
   "FavorStory_151204_Title": "When We Are Forgotten",
-  "FavorStory_151205_Content": "\"I don't know why that war started. What I do know is what it left behind, in me, and in the people I saw. All the wounds that won't heal...\"\nIn a classroom at <te href=851069>Startorch Academy</te>, Lily stood at the lectern, swallowed her tears, and set the sentence down in front of the students without letting her voice waver.\nNo murmur ran along the rows. Nobody clapped. The students had no idea what they were supposed to feel. Sympathy? Anger? Grief?\nThen Lucilla knocked on the door. \"That's all for today, everyone. Lily, come with me.\"\nLucilla walked her up to the President's office. It had always been Lily who brewed the tea, Lily who did the tending; that was what a doctor was for. At Startorch Academy she found herself on the other end of it.\nLucilla eased a cup of coffee across the desk to her. It must have been too hot; Lily flinched at the first mouthful and made a small noise like a startled squirrel.\n\"Careful. Too hot? Too bitter? I'm still no good at coffee, clearly. You don't have to force it down...\"\n\"I'm all right, President Lucilla.\"\n\"I don't just mean the coffee. I mean all of it, everything you've been carrying. If it hurts, you don't have to keep...\"\n\"No. This is something I have to do. I promised them I'd tell as many people as I could what that war did to the people in it, so that it never happens again.\"\nSometimes, Lucilla thought the girl before her was every bit as stubborn as she herself had once been, back in the days when she staked the fate of Startorch Academy on the table and stood against <te href=850074>Threnodian</te>.\n\"Well. All right, then.\"\n\"President, where are you going?\"\n\"Giving you some privacy. What I meant to say was: if it hurts, you don't have to keep telling it. And if you want to let it out anyway, then cry out loud. There is always some corner of the world that will take a person's crying. And don't worry, the President's office is very well soundproofed.\"\nThe door clicked shut, and the room was hers. She took the cup in both hands and smiled. The President probably never knew that Lily had made an agreement with herself: no more crying alone.\nAnd President Lucilla really was no good at coffee. Lily drank the cup down and found herself drowsy instead of sharp. She let go and went under on the sofa... and somewhere in the blur of the dream she came back to the Dream Abode, in the Somnoire.\n\"So you've come. You've got your color back. How is the world outside?\"\n\"Mm. Better than the dream. Gentler.\"\nLily told the keeper of the Dream Abode how her life at Startorch Academy had been.\n\"And are you happy now? Content? Does anything in you kindle at the thought of tomorrow, even a spark?\"\nLily nodded, and the keeper of the Dream Abode took the nod for an answer. It stretched itself out long, and nothing at all showed on its face.\n\"Good. It suits me too, your staying alive. So go on using those eyes of yours and see more of the real world for me. Now, time to wake.\"\nWhen she woke, Lucilla was already back at the desk, working.\n\"Ah. You're awake...\"\n\"Mm. Thank you, President Lucilla. I've been a trouble to all of you. I think it's time I was on my way.\"\n\"Have you decided where you'll go next?\"\n\"The New Federation... I want to look at it again, and get to know it properly this time.\"",
+  "FavorStory_151205_Content": "\"I don't know why that war started. What I do know is what it left behind, in me, and in the people I saw. All the wounds that won't heal...\"\nIn a classroom at <te href=851069>Startorch Academy</te>, Lily stood at the lectern, swallowed her tears, and set the sentence down in front of the students without letting her voice waver.\nNo murmur ran along the rows. Nobody clapped. The students had no idea what they were supposed to feel. Sympathy? Anger? Grief?\nThen Lucilla knocked on the door. \"That's all for today, everyone. Lily, come with me.\"\nLucilla walked her up to the President's office. It had always been Lily who brewed the tea, Lily who did the tending; that was what a doctor was for. At Startorch Academy she found herself on the other end of it.\nLucilla eased a cup of coffee across the desk to her. It must have been too hot; Lily flinched at the first mouthful and made a small noise like a startled squirrel.\n\"Careful. Too hot? Too bitter? I'm still no good at coffee, clearly. You don't have to force it down...\"\n\"I'm all right, President Lucilla.\"\n\"I don't just mean the coffee. I mean all of it, everything you've been carrying. If it hurts, you don't have to keep...\"\n\"No. This is something I have to do. I promised them I'd tell as many people as I could what that war did to the people in it, so that it never happens again.\"\nSometimes, Lucilla thought the girl before her was every bit as stubborn as she herself had once been, back in the days when she staked the fate of Startorch Academy on the table and stood against a <te href=850074>Threnodian</te>.\n\"Well. All right, then.\"\n\"President, where are you going?\"\n\"Giving you some privacy. What I meant to say was: if it hurts, you don't have to keep telling it. And if you want to let it out anyway, then cry out loud. There is always some corner of the world that will take a person's crying. And don't worry, the President's office is very well soundproofed.\"\nThe door clicked shut, and the room was hers. She took the cup in both hands and smiled. The President probably never knew that Lily had made an agreement with herself: no more crying alone.\nAnd President Lucilla really was no good at coffee. Lily drank the cup down and found herself drowsy instead of sharp. She let go and went under on the sofa... and somewhere in the blur of the dream she came back to the Dream Abode, in the Somnoire.\n\"So you've come. You've got your color back. How is the world outside?\"\n\"Mm. Better than the dream. Gentler.\"\nLily told the keeper of the Dream Abode how her life at Startorch Academy had been.\n\"And are you happy now? Content? Does anything in you kindle at the thought of tomorrow, even a spark?\"\nLily nodded, and the keeper of the Dream Abode took the nod for an answer. It stretched itself out long, and nothing at all showed on its face.\n\"Good. It suits me too, your staying alive. So go on using those eyes of yours and see more of the real world for me. Now, time to wake.\"\nWhen she woke, Lucilla was already back at the desk, working.\n\"Ah. You're awake...\"\n\"Mm. Thank you, President Lucilla. I've been a trouble to all of you. I think it's time I was on my way.\"\n\"Have you decided where you'll go next?\"\n\"The New Federation... I want to look at it again, and get to know it properly this time.\"",
   "FavorStory_151205_Title": "When We Reach for Tomorrow",
   "FavorStory_160101_Content": "\"Did you hear? That new Director at the <te href=850085>Ministry of Development</te>'s Defense Division doesn't let anything get past her!\" This rumor spread quickly through <te href=850091>Jinzhou</te>, soon becoming the city's hot topic.\n\nThe Ministry of Development ordered a batch of materials intended for the construction of a new defense line, but one container was filled with unlabeled, subpar goods. To complicate the matter even more, the batch had three different manufacturers, none willing to take responsibility for the defect.\n\n\"Director Taoqi, you have to help us sort this out. The Ministry of Development can't just accuse innocent people!\"\n\nThe three representatives said, their voices growing louder in an attempt to attract more attention.\n\nTaoqi calmly navigated through the commotion while softly putting off the noisy representatives. \"Alright, alright, I understand,\" even though she wasn't really paying attention to their complaints. She then crouched down to inspect the unlabeled container. After examining a few pieces of materials for a while, Taoqi seemed to have an idea. In a casual tone, she turned to the three representatives and said, \"But it seems one of you is lying. Or perhaps, all three of you are.\"\n\nAfter that, Taoqi and her junior, Xiaoyu, discreetly visited three manufacturing factories. They gathered clues about the machinery, transportation routes used that evening, and delivery schedules. With this information, Taoqi was able to solve the puzzle and ensure those who had been deceitful would face consequences.\n\nWithin days, rumors spread throughout the city. One of the three representatives was interrogated by Director Taoqi of the Ministry of Development. Then, news surfaced about another representative being caught, followed by the final one. Eventually, Jinzhou News broke a story revealing their collusion in supplying substandard materials. The public was left stunned, while only Taoqi and Xiaoyu knew the truth.\n\n\"I know! It-it's the typical prisoner's dilemma! You brought one of them in for a 'private talk' first, then you made sure the other two knew what happened. All of this was meant to put pressure on them so they'd betray and tell on each other, right? But how did you know they were all lying?\"\n\n\"People can lie, but machines can't. Each factory has its unique machine specifications. The answer was already clear when I examined those materials. Oh well... it did cost me several lunch breaks.\" Taoqi complained as she pulled her sleep mask down, determined to make up for those lost breaks.",
